# Elephant toothpaste: the soap catches oxygen; it does not make it
#
# Simulation activity. For a real child-facing activity, use only household
# 3% hydrogen peroxide with an adult, fitted goggles and gloves. Stronger
# peroxide is for virtual exploration here, not a home instruction.
register lv1

# Two equal peroxide portions make this a fair test. The first gets dish soap;
# the second is the no-soap control.
add v1 Wasserstoffperoxid_3% 100mL
add v1 Spülmittel 10mL
measure v1 thermometer

new beaker
add v2 Wasserstoffperoxid_3% 100mL
measure v2 thermometer

# Dry yeast is a catalase-activity surrogate in this first model. Warm water
# normally hydrates it in the real demonstration; brand, age and hydration
# change activity, so Kerotakis does not claim one universal yeast rate.
add v1 water 20mL @ 35C
add v1 Hefe 1g
stir v1
add v2 water 20mL @ 35C
add v2 Hefe 1g
stir v2

# One clock advances both beakers. Oxygen yield and reaction heat come from the
# same peroxide rate law. Only v1 has a declared foam stabilizer.
wait 1s
inspect v1
inspect v2
measure v1 thermometer
measure v2 thermometer

# Prediction to discuss: both vessels should make the same amount of oxygen
# from equal peroxide. v1 holds much of it temporarily as foam; v2 bubbles but
# cannot build persistent foam. Wait again to watch the modeled foam drain.
wait 180s
inspect v1
